module Core

# ============================================================================
# D64Trans — decimal64 (Pmax=16) transcendentals + libm surface via the
# DoubleDouble engine (DD, two-F64 unevaluated pair, ~106-bit significand ~31
# decimal digits >> 16). DD is the D64 (~54-bit) scratch engine: far cheaper than
# the 128-bit UnpackedFloat soft-float when only ~16 digits are needed. DD carries
# the FULL method surface incl. erf/erfc/tgamma/lgamma, so every transcendental
# unpacks D64 -> DD, calls the DD routine, and packs DD -> D64 (round to 16 digits).
# ============================================================================

# ============================================================================
# Radix adapters (dec <-> DD). A D64 value is coeff * 10^exp; DD holds hi+lo on
# hardware f64. Conversion is exact for the coefficient (a <=16-digit integer split
# into two <=8-digit halves, each < 2^53 so f64-exact) and faithful for the 10^exp
# scale (a two-level sqrt-split power-of-ten table, ~1 ULP of DD).
# ============================================================================

# ============================================================================
# Power-of-ten DD tables (sqrt-split, S=20). Four small tables of DD (hi,lo) pairs stored as their
# raw IEEE bit patterns (exact, no literal-parse rounding): HP=10^(20*hi) hi=0..20; LP=10^lo lo=0..19;
# HN=10^-(20*hi); LN=10^-lo. dd_pow10(e) = HP[|e|/20]*LP[|e|%20] (or HN/LN for e<0) — one DD multiply,
# zero division. Generated at ~120-digit precision, round-half-even; entries beyond f64's range are
# stored as +inf (10^320+) or 0 (10^-340-), matching the old binary-exp overflow/underflow boundary.
# ============================================================================
